          "source": {"type":"pr","repo":"adobe/spectrum-design-data","number":1524,"title":"feat(design-data): promote public Spectrum Hub guidance to main","url":"https://github.com/adobe/spectrum-design-data/pull/1524"},
          "entries": [
            {"id":"ec3376fa-0","kind":"document-block","change":"updated","class":"documentation","bump":"patch","indirect":false,"contexts":[],"record":"radio-button/documentBlocks/0","name":"guideline: Keyboard focus.\n\nA radio button can be navigated","fields":[{"path":"content","before":"Radio buttons present a list of options where only one can be selected at a time. They are styled consistently with other components that support binary selection. Use when all options should be visible and only one can apply.","after":"Keyboard focus.\n\nA radio button can be navigated using a keyboard. The keyboard focus state takes the radio button's visual hover state and adds a blue ring to the radio button in focus."}]},
            {"id":"ec3376fa-1","kind":"document-block","change":"updated","class":"documentation","bump":"patch","indirect":false,"contexts":[],"record":"radio-button/documentBlocks/1","name":"guideline: Text overflow.\n\nWhen the button text is too long","fields":[{"path":"content","before":"Radio button should always have a label for accessibility and clear comprehension. When the label is not defined, a radio button becomes standalone. Standalone radio buttons should only be used when their connection to other components is clear and they give sufficient context — for example, in application panels.","after":"Text overflow.\n\nWhen the button text is too long for the horizontal space available, it wraps to form another line."}]},
            {"id":"ec3376fa-2","kind":"document-block","change":"updated","class":"documentation","bump":"patch","indirect":false,"contexts":[],"record":"radio-button/documentBlocks/2","name":"guideline: Radio button, checkbox or switch?\n\nRadio buttons","fields":[{"path":"content","before":"By default, radio buttons are not emphasized. This version is optimal for when the radio button is not the core part of an interface, such as in application panels, where all visual components are monochrome in order to direct focus to the content.","after":"Radio button, checkbox or switch?\n\nRadio buttons are best when only one option can be selected at a time from a set (for example choosing one preference or mode). Switches are best for communicating activation (e.g., turning a setting on or off). Checkboxes are best for communicating selection (e.g., selecting multiple items from a list)."}]},
            {"id":"ec3376fa-3","kind":"document-block","change":"updated","class":"documentation","bump":"patch","indirect":false,"contexts":[],"record":"radio-button/documentBlocks/3","name":"guideline: When to use an emphasized radio button?\n\nEmphasi","fields":[{"path":"content","before":"The emphasized version provides a visual prominence that is optimal for forms, settings, lists or grids of assets, and other situations where a radio button needs to be noticed.","after":"When to use an emphasized radio button?\n\nEmphasized radio buttons are optimal for forms, settings, and other scenarios where the radio buttons need to be noticed. Default radio buttons are optimal for application panels where all the visual components are monochrome in order to direct focus to the canvas."}]},
            {"id":"ec3376fa-4","kind":"document-block","change":"updated","class":"documentation","bump":"patch","indirect":false,"contexts":[],"record":"radio-button/documentBlocks/4","name":"guideline: No partial state.\n\nRadio buttons can only be on ","fields":[{"path":"content","before":"Radio button can either be selected or not selected. They cannot be in an indeterminate state (unlike checkboxes).","after":"No partial state.\n\nRadio buttons can only be on or off. Indeterminate radio buttons don't exist in accessibility APIs, so it's not possible to make an indeterminate radio button accessible. If you need to show a partial state, use a checkbox instead of a radio button. When a parent radio button represents a group of radio buttons, it should be turned off unless all of the radio buttons are on (turning the parent radio button on turns all of the radio buttons on)."}]},
            {"id":"ec3376fa-5","kind":"document-block","change":"removed","class":"documentation","bump":"patch","indirect":false,"contexts":[],"record":"radio-button/documentBlocks/5","name":"guideline: Radio buttons come in four different sizes: smal","fields":[],"before":{"type":"guideline","content":"Radio buttons come in four different sizes: small, medium, large, and extra-large. The medium size is the default and most frequently used option. Use the other sizes sparingly; they should be used to create a hierarchy of importance within the page."}},
            {"id":"ec3376fa-6","kind":"document-block","change":"removed","class":"documentation","bump":"patch","indirect":false,"contexts":[],"record":"radio-button/documentBlocks/6","name":"guideline: A radio button can be navigated using a keyboard","fields":[],"before":{"type":"guideline","content":"A radio button can be navigated using a keyboard. The keyboard focus state takes the radio button’s visual hover state and adds a blue ring to the radio button in focus."}},
            {"id":"ec3376fa-7","kind":"document-block","change":"removed","class":"documentation","bump":"patch","indirect":false,"contexts":[],"record":"radio-button/documentBlocks/7","name":"guideline: When the button text is too long for the horizon","fields":[],"before":{"type":"guideline","content":"When the button text is too long for the horizontal space available, it wraps to form another line."}},
            {"id":"ec3376fa-8","kind":"document-block","change":"removed","class":"documentation","bump":"patch","indirect":false,"contexts":[],"record":"radio-button/documentBlocks/8","name":"guideline: Radio buttons are best when only one option can ","fields":[],"before":{"type":"guideline","content":"Radio buttons are best when only one option can be selected at a time from a set (for example choosing one preference or mode). Switches are best for communicating activation (e.g., turning a setting on or off). Checkboxes are best for communicating selection (e.g., selecting multiple items from a list)."}},
            {"id":"ec3376fa-9","kind":"document-block","change":"removed","class":"documentation","bump":"patch","indirect":false,"contexts":[],"record":"radio-button/documentBlocks/9","name":"guideline: Emphasized radio buttons are optimal for forms, ","fields":[],"before":{"type":"guideline","content":"Emphasized radio buttons are optimal for forms, settings, and other scenarios where the radio buttons need to be noticed. Default radio buttons are optimal for application panels where all the visual components are monochrome in order to direct focus to the canvas."}},
            {"id":"ec3376fa-10","kind":"document-block","change":"removed","class":"documentation","bump":"patch","indirect":false,"contexts":[],"record":"radio-button/documentBlocks/10","name":"guideline: Radio buttons can only be on or off. Indetermina","fields":[],"before":{"type":"guideline","content":"Radio buttons can only be on or off. Indeterminate radio buttons don’t exist in accessibility APIs, so it’s not possible to make an indeterminate radio button accessible. If you need to show a partial state, use a checkbox instead of a radio button. When a parent radio button represents a group of radio buttons, it should be turned off unless all of the radio buttons are on (turning the parent radio button on turns all of the radio buttons on)."}}
